  <title>Graphic symbols: ® © ™ UL CSA</title>

  <p><gui>+ Symbol ▾</gui> opens a list of the five symbols every Zebra
  printer carries, and adds the one you choose. In ZPL it is
  <code>^GS</code>, with a letter picking the symbol.</p>
  <list>
    <item><p>® Registered trademark</p></item>
    <item><p>© Copyright</p></item>
    <item><p>™ Trademark</p></item>
    <item><p>UL, the Underwriters Laboratories approval mark</p></item>
    <item><p>CSA, the Canadian Standards Association approval mark</p></item>
  </list>

  <terms>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Symbol</gui></title>
      <p>Which of the five to print.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Height</gui> and <gui>Width</gui></title>
      <p>The symbols come from a bitmap font whose cell is 24 dots square.
      Like the printer's other built-in fonts, it prints only at whole
      multiples of that cell, 1 to 10 times in each direction, so every
      symbol is 24, 48, 72 … up to 240 dots tall and wide. Height and width
      are separate, so a symbol can be stretched one way.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Orientation</gui> and <gui>Reverse</gui></title>
      <p>As for <link xref="text">text</link>. Turned 90° or 270°, the box's
      width and height swap.</p>
    </item>
  </terms>

  <section id="dragging">
    <title>Dragging a symbol's handles</title>
    <p>A drag asks for a size, and the symbol takes the largest whole step
    that fits inside the box you dragged, not the nearest. That way a
    symbol never grows past the room it was given: when the label is made
    smaller, or a group holding the symbol is scaled down, the symbol stays
    inside it.</p>
  </section>

  <note>
    <p>The printer draws these from a font of its own. The canvas shows
    LinuxZPL's drawings of them, at the size and place the printer prints
    them, so they may differ slightly in detail.</p>
  </note>
</page>
